<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khwarazm</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khwarazm is a historical region around the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> river and its delta south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and Turkmenistan. An ancient centre of irrigated agriculture and trade, it gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, whose shahs built a large state from this base in the twelfth and early thirteenth centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khwarazm.jpg" alt="Khwarazm" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HistoryofIran · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Khwarazm_in_the_Islamic_period,_before_the_Mongol_invasion.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The scholar al-Biruni, brought to Ghazni after Mahmud conquered <b>Khwarazm</b>, learned Sanskrit and wrote a detailed study of Indian religion, science and customs, completed around 1030.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Pastoral nomadism remained the basis of life on the steppe, with the khan's camp migrating seasonally along the Volga, but the khanate also ruled settled regions: the towns and farmland of Volga Bulgaria, <b>Khwarazm</b> with its city of Urgench, the Crimea, and the tribute-paying principalities of Rus'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Khwarazmian Empire arose in the late eleventh and twelfth centuries in <b>Khwarazm</b>, the oasis region along the lower Amu Darya, as a provincial polity under Seljuk rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Qatwan, Erbuz was sent in 1142 to pillage <b>Khwarazm</b> until Atsiz agreed to pay 30,000 dinars a year, and tribute remained the usual relation with Khwarazm, the Karakhanids and the Turkic leaders of Khorasan, who all paid to gain Khitan favour in the 1150s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Iraqi line staggered on through a succession of weak sultans dominated by their atabegs until Toghrul III was killed in battle by the <b>Khwarazm</b> Shah Tekish in 1194, and with him the Great Seljuk line ended entirely.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…with honey, wax, hides and slaves, were brought from the northern peoples whom Arab writers called the Wisu and the Yura, and from the Rus, and were exchanged at Bolghar for silver coins, textiles, glass and other goods from <b>Khwarazm</b>, the Samanid lands of Central Asia and Iran.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
